[%- endmacro -%]
{#- QS-372: the implement-variant rule, owned here once (create-plan's
    "Determine NEXT_PHASE", review-task step 6). `var` is `NEXT_PHASE` or
    `{{next_implement}}`, always assigned the bare phase name. The STOP
    message's prefix is stable so an automated caller can detect it. -#}
[%- macro implement_routing(var) -%]
Route by the **declared target** (QS-321 — never inferred from the
files you touch): re-run `python scripts/qs/context.py`. If `context.py`
exits non-zero / prints no JSON, or returns empty `labels` **and**
`title`, the `gh` lookup failed: **STOP** and print:

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: the context.py / gh lookup failed — retry, or tell me which variant to run.
```

(If `issue` is null — the branch carries no `QS_<N>` issue number — say
so instead of suggesting a retry.) Do **not** print the label message or
commands below; then wait for the user as described in the last
paragraph. Otherwise take
`target`. `factory` → `[[ var ]] = implement-setup-task`; `product` →
`[[ var ]] = implement-task`. For any other value (empty — no or
ambiguous `target:*` label), **STOP** and print (substitute `{{issue}}`
with the issue number from `context.py` in the messages and commands
below):

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: issue #{{issue}} has no single target:* label. Label it with exactly one of target:factory or target:product (see the commands below), or tell me which variant to run.
```

Print the commands below to the user as well. **Do NOT run any of them
yourself** — the target label is the user's declaration (QS-321). The
user runs exactly one:

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:factory
```

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:product
```

If `labels` already carries **both** target labels, tell the user to
remove the wrong one — `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:factory`
or `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:product` (the one that
is wrong; concrete labels only).

After printing (either STOP), wait for the user: if they say to retry or
report adding a label, re-run `context.py` and route again; if they name
`implement-task` / `implement-setup-task` (with or without a leading
`/`), or `factory` / `product` (map `factory` → `implement-setup-task`,
`product` → `implement-task`), use it (assign the bare name — drop any
leading `/`); any other answer → ask again.
[%- endmacro -%]
{#- QS-372: the phase handoff, written once for both harnesses. Call it as
    `[% call(prefix) m.handoff("<next_cmd>") %]` with banner lines
    (`[[ prefix ]] ...`) as the body. On Claude the user-facing block is the
    launcher's `handoff_text` (scripts/qs/launchers/claude.py), printed
    verbatim; on OpenCode the agent itself runs `new_context`, so its
    contract stays prose. `fix_plan` adds the review-fix flags and the
    existing-session block; `rerun` is the phase re-run after the fix. -#}
[% macro handoff(next_cmd, fix_plan=false, rerun="") %]
[% set banner = caller("✅" if harness == "claude" else "[OK]") | trim %]
**Before running** — substitute `{{worktree}}`, `{{issue}}`, `{{title}}`[[ ",\n`{{fix_plan_path}}`, `{{pr_number}}`" if fix_plan else "" ]]
with the values you captured earlier[[ (";\nsubstitute `" ~ next_cmd ~ "` with the value you resolved above") if next_cmd.startswith("{{") else "" ]].

```bash
python scripts/qs/next_step.py \
    --next-cmd "[[ next_cmd ]]" \
    --work-dir "{{worktree}}" \
    --issue {{issue}} \
    --title "{{title}}" \
[% if fix_plan %]
    --fix-plan-path "{{fix_plan_path}}" \
    --pr-number {{pr_number}} \
[% endif %]
    --harness [[ "claude-code" if harness == "claude" else "opencode" ]]
```

[% if harness == "claude" %]
Parse the JSON. If the command exits non-zero or prints no JSON, if the
JSON contains an `error` key, or if it has no `handoff_text`, STOP and
print the raw output verbatim. Otherwise print the report below,
replacing `<handoff_text>` with the payload's `handoff_text` **verbatim**
(do not rephrase, reorder or drop any part of it):

```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner ]]

[% endif %]
<handoff_text>
[% if rerun %]

Then re-run /[[ rerun ]] (or open a fresh `claude --agent qs-[[ rerun ]]`
session) to verify.
[% endif %]
```
[% else %]
Parse the JSON output of ``next_step.py``.

**If `next_step.py` exits non-zero or prints no JSON, or its JSON has no
`new_context`**, STOP and print the raw output verbatim.

**If the `next_step.py` JSON contains an `error` key**, STOP and print
the raw JSON to the user. Do not proceed to run `new_context`.

[% if fix_plan %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string and the
``existing_session_prompt`` value. The ``existing_session_prompt`` is
a paste-into-already-running-session prompt — it is NOT a
session-spawn command. Do NOT execute it. **If
`existing_session_prompt` is missing or null, omit the "Already
running an implementation session?" block from the user report;
only emit it when the field is a non-empty string**.
[% else %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string.
[% endif %]

**Run `new_context` via the Bash tool**. The string is a
``python scripts/qs/spawn_session.py --agent qs-<phase> --directory
<wd> --title ... --prompt ...`` invocation — already inside the
allow-listed ``python scripts/qs/*`` pattern. Do NOT extract only the
prompt and send it to the current session. Do NOT strip
``--agent qs-<phase>``. The ``--agent`` flag is what binds the
next-phase orchestrator to the new session via OpenCode's HTTP API
``POST /session/<id>/prompt_async`` body — strip it and the prompt
lands on the default agent, breaking the pipeline silently.

**If the Bash tool returns an error before producing any JSON output**
(e.g., permission denied, missing interpreter), STOP and print the
Bash tool's error message verbatim. Do not attempt to parse JSON.

Parse the stdout of that command as JSON. The success contract is
**binary**:

- ``status == "session_created"`` AND ``agent`` equals `qs-` followed
  by the phase name passed to `--next-cmd` (here: `qs-[[ next_cmd ]]`)
  → success; report to the user:

  ```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner | indent(2, first=true) ]]
[% endif %]
  [OK] Next phase session created: qs-[[ next_cmd ]]
       (visible in the OpenCode session list on the left)
[% if fix_plan %]

  Already running an implementation session?
  Paste this prompt into it:
    {{existing_session_prompt}}
[% endif %]
[% if rerun %]

  Then re-activate `qs-[[ rerun ]]` (or open a fresh session bound to
  it) to verify.
[% endif %]
  ```
[% if fix_plan %]

  (Omit the "Already running an implementation session?" block when
  `existing_session_prompt` is missing or null.)
[% endif %]

- **Anything else** (any other ``status`` value, missing or mismatched
  ``agent`` field, non-zero exit code, malformed JSON) → STOP. Print
  the raw JSON output verbatim to the user. Do NOT claim the next
  phase started. The user inspects the JSON and acts on the specific
  failure mode (``agent_file_missing``, ``agent_file_unreadable``,
  ``agent_file_empty``, ``worktree_invalid``, ``fallback_cli``,
  ``fallback_unavailable``, ``session_orphaned`` — each documented in
  ``scripts/qs/spawn_session.py``).
[% endif %]
[% endmacro %]
{#- QS-453: the review-round protocol shared by the two code-review
    orchestrators (qs-review-task, qs-verify-task): open the round, delta
    diff, consolidation keys, the finding-verifier post-pass, recording in
    the ledger, the fast path, triage buckets and decisions. Written once so
    the two orchestrators cannot drift; each template keeps its own roster,
    doc-maintenance audit, PR comments and handoffs. -#}
[%- macro review_ledger_intro() -%]
**The review ledger (QS-453).**
`docs/stories/QS-{{issue}}.review_ledger.json` is this PR's durable
record of review findings, decisions and state history. Write it
**only** through `python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py` (single writer —
never edit it by hand). Every subcommand takes `--issue {{issue}}` and
prints JSON: exit 0 = ok; exit 1 = invalid input or wrong lifecycle
state (`{"error": ...}`); exit 2 = corrupt ledger — HALT and show the
error. A round is *finished* once the ledger is committed; a dirty
ledger is an unfinished round.
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_open_round() -%]
Sync the heads first:

```bash
git fetch origin {{branch}}
gh pr view {{pr_number}} --json headRefOid --jq .headRefOid
git log -1 --format=%H
git status --porcelain
```

Compare the PR head (`headRefOid`) with the local head
(`git merge-base --is-ancestor <a> <b>` tells ahead from behind):

- equal → go on;
- local ahead of the PR head (a failed push) → `git push origin
  {{branch}}`, then re-read the PR head;
- local behind the PR head → STOP: "pull first" (`git pull --ff-only`);
- diverged → STOP and ask the user.

The worktree must equal that head: the repo-reading reviewers and the
finding verifier read it. If `git status --porcelain` lists anything
besides `docs/stories/QS-{{issue}}.review_ledger.json` and
`docs/stories/QS-{{issue}}.story_review_fix_*`, STOP and ask the user to
commit or stash it first.

Only then read the PR and its diff — on the synced head:

```bash
gh pr view {{pr_number}}
gh pr diff {{pr_number}}
```

Then open the round on that head (`<head>` below):

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py start-round --issue {{issue}} --head <head>
```

Add `--force-full` only when the user asked for a full review. Read the
JSON:

- `discarded: true` → tell the user "round N was not finished — redoing
  it".
- `stale_fix_plans` non-empty → ask the user whether to delete them
  (fix plans of an unfinished round, never committed: untracked, or
  staged by a commit that failed — unstage those with
  `git rm -q --cached -- <path>` before deleting the file).
- `mode: full` with a non-null `reason` → report the reason.
- exit 1 `"round N committed unfinished"` (the ledger was committed
  with `open` findings) → STOP and show the listed IDs. The user decides
  each one with `review_ledger.py set` (it works on that committed
  round), then `check-round`, and finish that round exactly like step 8,
  with `{{round}}` = the error's `round`: any finding `accepted` with that
  round as `decided_round` (decided before or during the recovery) →
  write the fix plan for them, commit it with the ledger and hand off to
  implement (a restart would only see "no-change" and drop the fix);
  none → commit the ledger with the ledger-only fence and restart at
  step 1. Recovery decisions already recorded but not committed are
  kept (the error then says "recovery decisions are not committed yet")
  — finish and commit them.
- `status: no-change` → say nothing changed since round N and offer a
  forced full re-review (`--force-full`); otherwise stop.
- otherwise keep `round` (`{{round}}` below), `mode` and `base`. If the
  ledger already has findings, show it:

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py show --issue {{issue}} --format md
```
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_delta_diff() -%]
The delta diff (pasted or passed to the delta reviewers, and given to
the verifier in step 4):

```bash
git diff <base>..<head> -- . ':(exclude)docs/stories/QS-{{issue}}.review_ledger.json' ':(exclude)docs/stories/QS-{{issue}}.story_review_fix_*'
```

Rounds 1–2 are always full; a forced, non-ancestor or merge-crossing
round is full too (`start-round` says why).
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_consolidate() -%]
Deduplicate across reviewers (`file:line` + similar text → one entry)
and key each consolidated finding `C-1`, `C-2`, … with its source (the
reviewer's agent name — a co-sourced finding keeps the first and names
the others in its body), severity (`must-fix` / `should-fix` /
`nice-to-have`), `where` (repo-relative `path:line`), title and body.

- Drop as **invalid** any finding whose `where` is the review ledger or
  a fix plan.
- The "CodeRabbit unavailable" meta-finding is an info line for the
  user, never a finding.
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_verify() -%]
Spawn `qs-review-finding-verifier` **once**, after the fan-out and the
consolidation — a sequential post-pass, not a parallel reviewer. Pass
it:

- PR number, worktree path, head SHA;
- the diff reviewed this round (full, or the delta diff) — above 3,000
  lines, only the changed-file list plus the hunks of the files the
  findings cite (it reads the rest itself);
- the consolidated findings (key, source, severity, where, title,
  body);
- the ledger, with each decided finding's file changes since its
  decision:

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py show --issue {{issue}} --format json --head <head>
```

It answers with exactly one fenced `json` block holding one object
(`"mode": "verified"`): per finding a verdict, `reproduces_when`,
`severity_action` and ledger `relation`; one check per `accepted`
ledger finding.

**Fallback** — the verifier crashed, returned no JSON block, or `add`
rejected its object a second time (step 5): build the object yourself,
verifying nothing — `{"mode": "fallback", "checks": [], "findings":
[...]}`, each finding with its `key`, `source`, `severity`, `where`,
`title` copied and `"verdict": null`, `"evidence": null`,
`"reproduces_when": null`, `"severity_action": "keep"`,
`"relation": {"kind": "NEW", "ref": null}`, `"note": "unverified"`.
Fallback findings are never refuted, and accepted ledger findings stay
`accepted` (unchecked this round).
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_record() -%]
Paste the block's **inner** JSON (without the fence) into `add`, and
pass every consolidated key with `--keys` — `add` rejects an object that
drops or invents a finding. With no consolidated findings, pass
`--keys ""` (the round still records its checks):

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py add --issue {{issue}} --keys <C-1,C-2,…> <<'QS_LEDGER'
<the JSON object>
QS_LEDGER
```

- exit 0 → keep the per-key `effect` (`new`, `new-downgraded`,
  `duplicate`, `reopened`, `challenged`, `flip-flop`, `merged`) and the
  per-ID `checks` results (`resolved`, `reopened`, `merged`).
- exit 2 → HALT and show the error (corrupt ledger).
- exit 1 on the verifier's object → show the error and re-ask the
  verifier once with it; a second exit 1 → fall back (step 4).
- exit 1 on the fallback object, or a lifecycle error → HALT with the
  error. You never edit findings to make `add` pass.

Then load the round's findings (state, severity, verdict, evidence,
note, relation, history):

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py show --issue {{issue}} --format json
```
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_fast_path() -%]
Take this path only in `verified` mode (there `accepted_unchecked` is
always empty — every accepted finding was checked; a `fallback` round
always goes to triage). If every `open` finding falls in the
`Dropped by verifier` or `Nice-to-have` bucket of step 7 — none in
`FLIP-FLOP`, `Reopened`, `Unverified` or `Blocking` — reject them with
their defaults:

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py set --issue {{issue}} --id <F-x> --to rejected --by verifier
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py set --issue {{issue}} --id <F-y> --to rejected --by default-skip
```

(`--by verifier` for `Dropped by verifier`, `--by default-skip` for
`Nice-to-have`).
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_triage() -%]
Place each `open` finding in exactly one bucket — the first row that
matches (severities are the ledger's `severity`, i.e. after the
verifier's `severity_action`):

| # | bucket | content | default |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `FLIP-FLOP` | effects `flip-flop` / `challenged`, shown with F-x's history | none — explicit decision |
| 2 | `Reopened` | effect `reopened` or check `reopened` | fix |
| 3 | `Unverified` | `mode: fallback` must-fix / should-fix | none — explicit decision |
| 4 | `Dropped by verifier` | verdict `REFUTED` or `severity_action: demote`, shown with evidence + note | reject (overridable) |
| 5 | `Blocking` | must-fix / should-fix with verdict `CONFIRMED`, `PLAUSIBLE` (marked `?`), or doc-maintenance (marked `deterministic`); promoted marked `↑` | fix |
| 6 | `Nice-to-have` | the rest, collapsed | reject (opt-in) |

Info lines, not buckets: "N repeats of decided findings, suppressed"
(`duplicate` effects), every `merged` and `new-downgraded` effect with
its history note, and — after step 8's `check-round` — any
`accepted_unchecked` IDs.

Present the summary:

```text
Findings for PR #{{pr_number}}, round {{round}} (<mode>):
  FLIP-FLOP: A  Reopened: B  Unverified: C
  Dropped by verifier: D  Blocking: E  Nice-to-have: F (collapsed)
```

Ask: "fix all / skip all / one by one?". "fix all" covers rows 2 + 5
(`Reopened` + `Blocking`) and keeps the defaults of rows 4 and 6;
"skip all" skips rows 2 + 5 and keeps the same defaults. `FLIP-FLOP`
and `Unverified` findings are never covered by either: walk each one,
show its ledger history, and ask the user to decide (fix or skip). One
by one: walk each finding, ask "fix or skip?" (a `Nice-to-have` is
fixed only when the user picks it; a `Dropped by verifier` default can
be overridden). Collect all decisions, then ask "confirm decisions?".
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_decisions() -%]
Record every decision — `FLIP-FLOP` decisions first:

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py set --issue {{issue}} --id <F-x> --to accepted
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py set --issue {{issue}} --id <F-x> --to rejected --by user
```

`--by user` for the user's explicit skips; `--by verifier` /
`--by default-skip` for the `Dropped by verifier` / `Nice-to-have`
defaults kept as-is. Accepting a `FLIP-FLOP` finding supersedes the one
it undoes (`superseded` in the output) — even when that one was reopened
this round, so a later `set` on it is refused because it is
`superseded`: that is expected, and it drops out of the fix plan (never
"fix A" and "undo A" together). The fix plan lists exactly the findings
`show` reports `accepted` with this round as `decided_round`. Then:

```bash
python scripts/qs/review_ledger.py check-round --issue {{issue}}
```

Exit 1 → some findings are still `open`: back to step 7 for the listed
IDs. Exit 0 → report any `accepted_unchecked` IDs; if there are some
and the user chose no fix, ask before routing to finish-task.
[%- endmacro -%]
[%- macro review_round_notes() -%]
Each ledger push re-runs CI (accepted). A crash before the commit
leaves the ledger dirty; the next `start-round` discards it and the
round is redone.
[%- endmacro -%]
